workedExamples.question_text:
Quadrilateral $PQRS$ has $PQ=RS=10$ cm and $QR=SP=6$ cm. Using diagonal $PR$, prove that $PQRS$ is a parallelogram.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},line width=0.7pt]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);\coordinate (Q) at (4,0);\coordinate (R) at (5.2,2.4);\coordinate (S) at (1.2,2.4);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(S)--cycle;
\draw (P)--(R);
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$} (Q) node[below right] {$Q$} (R) node[above right] {$R$} (S) node[above left] {$S$};
\path (2,0) node[below] {$10\text{ cm}$} (3.2,2.4) node[above] {$10\text{ cm}$} (0.6,1.2) node[left] {$6\text{ cm}$} (4.6,1.2) node[right] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The diagonals $KM$ and $LN$ of quadrilateral $KLMN$ intersect at $O$. Given $KO=MO=6$ cm and $LO=NO=4$ cm, prove that $KLMN$ is a parallelogram without assuming any sides are parallel.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},line width=0.7pt]
\coordinate (K) at (0,0);\coordinate (L) at (4,0);\coordinate (M) at (5.2,2.4);\coordinate (N) at (1.2,2.4);\coordinate (O) at (2.6,1.2);
\draw (K)--(L)--(M)--(N)--cycle;
\draw (K)--(M) (L)--(N);
\path (K) node[below left] {$K$} (L) node[below right] {$L$} (M) node[above right] {$M$} (N) node[above left] {$N$} (O) node[above,yshift=3mm] {$O$};
\path (1.3,0.6) node[below right] {$6\text{ cm}$} (3.9,1.8) node[above left] {$6\text{ cm}$} (3.3,0.6) node[above right] {$4\text{ cm}$} (1.9,1.8) node[below left] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
In quadrilateral $ABCD$, both pairs of opposite sides are equal: $AB = DC$ and $AD = BC$.
The diagonal $AC$ is drawn.
Prove that $ABCD$ is a parallelogram.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,decorations.markings,calc,angles,quotes}
\tikzset{sa/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\arrow{Stealth}}}}},da/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.4 with {\arrow{Stealth}},mark=at position 0.56 with {\arrow{Stealth}}}}},tk/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tk2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (C) at (5.6,2.3);
\coordinate (D) at (1.4,2.3);
\draw[tk] (A)--(B);
\draw[tk2] (B)--(C);
\draw[tk] (D)--(C);
\draw[tk2] (A)--(D);
\draw (A)--(C);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The diagonals of quadrilateral $ABCD$ meet at $E$ and bisect each other, so $AE = CE$ and $BE = DE$.
Prove that $ABCD$ is a parallelogram.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta,decorations.markings,calc,angles,quotes}
\tikzset{sa/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\arrow{Stealth}}}}},da/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.4 with {\arrow{Stealth}},mark=at position 0.56 with {\arrow{Stealth}}}}},tk/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tk2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (C) at (5.6,2.3);
\coordinate (D) at (1.4,2.3);
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (B)--(C);
\draw (C)--(D);
\draw (D)--(A);
\coordinate (E) at (2.8,1.15);
\draw[tk] (A)--(E);
\draw[tk] (E)--(C);
\draw[tk2] (B)--(E);
\draw[tk2] (E)--(D);
\node[above left] at (2.95,1.3) {$E$};
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
